    {
      "publisher": "Elsevier BV",
      "abstract": "<jats:p>Analog compute-in-memory design treats subarray parallel read (PR) as a throughput lever, yet the minimum ADC precision that keeps a chosen PR usable is rarely stated as a rule. Using DNN+NeuroSim V1.5 we map the PR×ADC space for ResNet18 on CIFAR-10/100, with a VGG8 control. At ADC=5, accuracy softens from PR=80 and has collapsed by PR=96 (27.41%) and PR=128 (10.00%), the latter a degenerate single-class output. At PR=128, six bits rather than the commonly assumed eight restore full-test accuracy (90.08%) at 26.98 TOPS/W, twice the efficiency of ADC=8 in 0.7× the area. That conclusion depends on how the converter is costed. NeuroSim&amp;apos;s stock levelOutput fixes sense-amp cost independently of the ADC width swept, so ADC=6, 8 and 12 all return 20.04 TOPS/W, overstating matched ADC=12 by 17× and hiding the ADC=6 peak. We therefore re-cost every hardware claim. Pre-ADC instrumentation ties the collapse to partition-MAC saturation. CIFAR-100 and VGG8 fail the same way, so the rule is not residual-specific, and the clipping is concentrated in a few layers, so converter bits assigned by clip rate alone target the wrong ones. We recommend PR64/ADC5 (28.0 TOPS/W), or PR128/ADC6 when a high read width is required, and reject ADC=5 for PR≥96.</jats:p>",
      "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.7385571",
